::ALG2_U03_L01_GP01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L01_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify &radic;50. {
= 5&radic;2#Correct. 50 = 25 &middot; 2, and &radic;25 = 5.
~ 25&radic;2#The square root of 25 is 5, not 25.
~ 2&radic;25#The perfect-square factor should be rooted.
~ 10&radic;5#This does not square back to 50.
}

::ALG2_U03_L01_GP02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L01_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Rewrite x<sup>2/3</sup> in radical form. {
~ &radic;(x<sup>3</sup>)#This reverses the numerator and denominator.
= &radic;<sup>3</sup>(x<sup>2</sup>)#Correct. The denominator 3 is the root index and the numerator 2 is the power.
~ &radic;<sup>2</sup>(x<sup>3</sup>)#This uses the numerator as the root index.
~ 3x<sup>2</sup>#A rational exponent does not become multiplication by the denominator.
}

::ALG2_U03_L01_GP03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L01_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 125<sup>1/3</sup>. {
~ 25#This is not the cube root of 125.
~ 15#This is not a root of 125.
= 5#Correct. 5<sup>3</sup> = 125.
~ 3#The denominator tells the root index, not the answer.
}

::ALG2_U03_L01_GP04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L01_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Simplify &radic;108. {
~ 18&radic;6#The square root of 36 is 6, not 36 or 18.
~ 3&radic;12#This is not fully simplified because 12 has a square factor.
~ 54&radic;2#This does not simplify the square factor correctly.
= 6&radic;3#Correct. 108 = 36 &middot; 3, so &radic;108 = 6&radic;3.
}

::ALG2_U03_L01_GP05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U03_L01_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.AE.7; MLA.A2.AE.8<br><br>Question:<br>Evaluate 16<sup>3/4</sup>. {
= 8#Correct. The fourth root of 16 is 2, and 2<sup>3</sup> = 8.
~ 12#This multiplies the exponent parts instead of applying a root and power.
~ 64#This uses 4 cubed but ignores the fourth root.
~ 4#This stops at the fourth root and does not apply the power 3.
}

